<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:orientation="horizontal"
    android:background="@color/tp_bg_home"
    android:padding="14dp"
    android:baselineAligned="false">

    <!-- LEFT: logo + status + Quick Controls. Scrolls instead of silently squashing the last
         button to nothing when the card's content doesn't fit the screen's vertical budget —
         LinearLayout has no built-in overflow handling of its own (bit us when a 4th Quick
         Controls button was added: it measured down to ~1dp tall instead of just running off
         the bottom, since its wrap_content child got an AT_MOST spec capped to whatever sliver
         of space was left).

         ⚠ HEIGHT BUDGET — specification §3.4. THE SCROLLVIEW IS LOAD-BEARING.
         DO NOT REMOVE IT to make this column look like the ENTER FLIGHT card beside it.

         ✔ MEASURED ON THE RC PLUS 2, 2026-08-20. The earlier budget here was arithmetic
         against a phone, written with no device attached, and two of its numbers were wrong:
         the buttons render 36dp tall, not the 48dp platform default it assumed, and the gaps
         between them render about 20dp where the layout asks for 8dp — the Button background
         carries its own inset.

         What the screen actually gives this column: the window is 1160px tall and the root
         padding takes 14dp from each end, so the content area ends at y=1110px.

         Before this change STOP/QUIT started at y=1109px — one pixel above the fold, thus
         entirely off screen, and the operator had to scroll to reach it. It was NOT reachable
         without scrolling on the device this application ships to.

         About 48dp was taken back, all of it from padding and gaps:

             root padding      20 -> 14   (12dp, both ends)
             card padding      14 -> 10   ( 8dp, both ends)
             card marginTop    12 ->  8   ( 4dp)
             button margins  10/8/8/8 -> 6/4/4/4  (16dp)
             divider marginTop 18 -> 12   ( 6dp)
             STOP/QUIT margin  12 -> 10   ( 2dp)

         ⚠ NO BUTTON GOT SHORTER. They are already 36dp, below the 48dp touch guideline, and
         this screen is used in gloves. Take space from gaps, never from a target.

         ⚠ The divider and gap above STOP/QUIT are TIGHTENED, NOT REMOVED. They exist because
         it is the only irreversible control on the card — see its own note below.

         Adding a row here: measure it on the device, do not re-derive it. If the column
         overflows again the ScrollView still catches it, and that is the point of keeping it.
         The Autel sibling, which is the base, wraps ITS left column in a ScrollView too.
         The specification's "fixed-height columns with weighted spacers" is about the ENTER
         FLIGHT card, which is `match_parent` with a weighted spacer further down this file. -->
    <ScrollView
        android:layout_width="0dp"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:layout_weight="1.2"
        android:fillViewport="true">
    <LinearLayout
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:orientation="vertical"
        android:paddingEnd="20dp">

        <!-- Logo + app name + TAK connection indicator -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="horizontal"
            android:gravity="center_vertical"
            android:layout_marginBottom="6dp">
            <ImageView
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="64dp"
                android:src="@drawable/takpilot2_logo"
                android:layout_marginEnd="12dp" />
            <LinearLayout
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:orientation="vertical">
                <!-- Bound to the same string as the launcher label so the two can't drift
                     apart the next time the app is renamed. -->
                <TextView
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:maxLines="1"
                    android:text="@string/sample_app_name"
                    android:textColor="@color/tp_text_primary"
                    android:textSize="22sp"
                    android:textStyle="bold" />
                <!-- Which exact build this phone is running. Build TIME, not just version, so
                     two sideloads made minutes apart in a debugging session can be told apart.
                     Sits where the TAK indicator used to: that moved to the ENTER FLIGHT card,
                     which is where a pilot actually acts on it. Matches the Autel sibling. -->
                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/homeVersion"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="6dp"
                    android:text="v— · built —"
                    android:textColor="@color/tp_text_secondary"
                    android:textSize="13sp" />
            </LinearLayout>
        </LinearLayout>

        <!-- Quick Controls -->
        <LinearLayout style="@style/homeCard">
            <TextView style="@style/homeCardTitle" android:text="Quick Controls" />
            <Button
                android:id="@+id/homeTakSetup"
                style="@style/TakButton.Primary"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="6dp"
                android:text="Pre-Flight Setup" />
            <Button
                android:id="@+id/homeFieldGuide"
                style="@style/TakButton.Info"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="4dp"
                android:text="Field Guide" />
            <Button
                android:id="@+id/homeDataSync"
                style="@style/TakButton.Neutral"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="4dp"
                android:text="Data Sync" />
            <Button
                android:id="@+id/homeDebugLog"
                style="@style/TakButton.Neutral"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="4dp"
                android:text="Debug Log" />

            <!-- STOP / QUIT LAST, BEHIND A DIVIDER AND A GAP.
                 It used to sit second, between Pre-Flight Setup and Field Guide — in the middle
                 of a run of same-shaped navigation buttons. A pilot reaching for a menu row in a
                 hurry could tear down the TAK connection and kill the app instead. It is the only
                 irreversible thing on this card, so it is separated from the things that merely
                 navigate. The confirm dialog is unchanged; this makes the mis-tap less likely
                 rather than relying on the dialog to catch it. -->
            <View
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="1dp"
                android:layout_marginTop="12dp"
                android:background="@color/tp_border" />
            <Button
                android:id="@+id/homeQuit"
                style="@style/TakButton.Danger"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="10dp"
                android:text="STOP / QUIT" />
        </LinearLayout>

    </LinearLayout>
    </ScrollView>

    <!-- RIGHT: the whole card IS the Enter Flight button (clickable, ripple feedback) -->
    <LinearLayout
        android:id="@+id/homeEnterFlight"
        android:layout_width="0dp"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:layout_weight="1"
        android:orientation="vertical"
        android:gravity="center_horizontal"
        android:background="@drawable/home_flight_card_clickable"
        android:clickable="true"
        android:focusable="true"
        android:padding="24dp">

        <View android:layout_width="0dp" android:layout_height="0dp" />

        <!-- The TAK logo was here: 72dp plus 4dp/8dp of margin, 84dp of this card's height.
             Removed 2026-08-20 to give that height back to information (operator). The mark is
             already on the header at the top left of this same screen, so nothing is lost by
             taking it off the card — it was decoration in the one place the card has to carry
             the aircraft, the link and the TAK state.

             ⚠ 84dp is now free here. Spend it on a readout, not on putting the logo back. -->

        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="ENTER FLIGHT"
            android:textColor="@color/tp_text_primary"
            android:textSize="26sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:letterSpacing="0.05" />

        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="Live FPV · Telemetry · TAK"
            android:textColor="@color/tp_accent"
            android:textSize="15sp"
            android:layout_marginTop="6dp" />

        <!-- TAK connection status — moved here from under the app name. This card is what a
             pilot taps to fly, so the one thing that decides whether the team will see them
             belongs where they are about to act on it, not on the screen's other side. -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="horizontal"
            android:gravity="center_vertical"
            android:layout_marginTop="10dp">
            <View
                android:id="@+id/homeTakDot"
                android:layout_width="10dp"
                android:layout_height="10dp"
                android:layout_marginEnd="6dp"
                android:background="@drawable/tak_status_dot" />
            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/homeTakStatus"
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:text="TAK: Disconnected"
                android:textColor="@color/tp_text_secondary"
                android:textSize="13sp" />
        </LinearLayout>

        <!-- Network state, directly under the TAK line it explains. The TAK dot alone cannot
             separate "server down" from "this phone has no network", and that ambiguity is what
             had pilots chasing enrollment failures on the sibling. Green means reachability was
             CONFIRMED, not merely that something is attached. -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="horizontal"
            android:gravity="center_vertical"
            android:layout_marginTop="4dp">
            <View
                android:id="@+id/homeNetworkDot"
                android:layout_width="10dp"
                android:layout_height="10dp"
                android:layout_marginEnd="6dp"
                android:background="@drawable/tak_status_dot" />
            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/homeNetwork"
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:text="Network: —"
                android:textColor="@color/tp_text_secondary"
                android:textSize="13sp" />
        </LinearLayout>

        <!-- Aircraft info, centered under the title -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="vertical"
            android:gravity="center_horizontal"
            android:layout_marginTop="12dp">
            <TextView style="@style/homeCardTitle" android:text="Aircraft"
                android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal" />
            <TextView android:id="@+id/homeAircraft" style="@style/homeCardValue"
                android:text="—" android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal" />
            <TextView android:id="@+id/homeSdk" style="@style/homeCardSub"
                android:text="MSDK —" android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal" />
            <!-- The two battery levels READ BACK FROM THE AIRCRAFT, not the saved
                 preference. They decide how long this flight can last, which is a pre-flight
                 decision, and reading the aircraft's own values is the only way to catch an
                 Apply that silently did not take. On the Mini 2 they cannot be changed at all
                 — it warns at 20% and lands at 10% whatever Pre-Flight says — so this line is
                 the pilot's only statement of what will actually happen.

                 THIS IS THE ONE STATUS FIELD THE PHONE KEEPS beyond the first five
                 (specification §3.1).

                 ⚠ THE OLD NOTE HERE SAID THESE ROWS "DO NOT FIT THIS CARD ON A 411dp SCREEN"
                 AND THAT ABOUT 16dp OF SLACK REMAINED. That was measured on a phone, and this
                 tree has ONE target — the RC Plus 2, 768x480dp landscape. Measured from a
                 screenshot of this very card on 2026-08-20: the empty band between the last
                 readout and TAP TO ENTER is 165dp, room for about nine rows. The premise was
                 inherited, not checked, which is the same mistake conformance V11 recorded.
                 Measure before trusting a height claim in this file. -->
            <TextView android:id="@+id/homeBatteryLevels" style="@style/homeCardSub"
                android:text="" android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal"
                android:layout_marginTop="2dp" android:textStyle="bold" />

            <!-- WHERE THE CAMERA WILL WRITE. Red means no recording will happen — the target
                 is internal memory, or the card is missing, full or unusable. See
                 AircraftStorage for why this readout exists at all. -->
            <TextView android:id="@+id/homeStorage" style="@style/homeCardSub"
                android:text="" android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal"
                android:layout_marginTop="2dp" android:textStyle="bold" />

            <!-- The rest of the pre-flight readiness block, matching the Autel sibling. Each
                 row reports the AIRCRAFT's answer; amber is "not read yet" and must never be
                 painted as a working value. -->
            <TextView android:id="@+id/homeAvoidance" style="@style/homeCardSub"
                android:text="" android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal"
                android:layout_marginTop="2dp" />

            <TextView android:id="@+id/homeSignalLoss" style="@style/homeCardSub"
                android:text="" android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal"
                android:layout_marginTop="2dp" />

            <TextView android:id="@+id/homeStickMode" style="@style/homeCardSub"
                android:text="" android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal"
                android:layout_marginTop="2dp" />

            <TextView android:id="@+id/homeControlResponse" style="@style/homeCardSub"
                android:text="" android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal"
                android:layout_marginTop="2dp" />

            <!-- Shown while the aircraft has not finished reporting. It is a HOLD, not a
                 spinner: a pilot who reads a half-arrived card can act on a value that is
                 about to change. Hidden the moment every row above has an answer. -->
            <TextView android:id="@+id/homeInitializing" style="@style/homeCardSub"
                android:text="READING AIRCRAFT…" android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal"
                android:layout_marginTop="4dp" android:textStyle="bold"
                android:textColor="@color/tp_state_unknown" android:visibility="gone" />
        </LinearLayout>

        <!-- Spacer pushes the tap hint to the bottom -->
        <View android:layout_width="0dp" android:layout_height="0dp"
            android:layout_weight="1" />

        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_marginBottom="4dp"
            android:text="TAP TO ENTER ›"
            android:textColor="@color/tp_accent"
            android:textSize="14sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:letterSpacing="0.08" />
    </LinearLayout>
</LinearLayout>
